List every setting a browser can open, and open every one it lists

v4.190.1 shipped this patch listing six sections in the tile's settings nav whose
panes `Settings.tsx` does not render. Clicking Orca Account, Mobile, Browser, SSH
Hosts, Computer Use or Plugins in the tile landed on a blank page. Confirmed on
the deployed 4.190.1 workspace: each nav row is present, and the pane area holds
the sidebar and nothing else.

The cause is that the decision lives in two files. The nav list is built in
`useSettingsNavigationMetadata.ts`; the panes are rendered in `Settings.tsx`
behind its own copy of `showDesktopOnlySettings = !isWebClient`. Changing one
without the other is what produced the blank pages, and nothing checked them
against each other.

Both files now answer the same way, and `settings-nav-render-parity.test.ts`
reads them together so they cannot drift again: it pairs the ids in
`Settings.tsx` with the JSX conditions wrapping them and fails on any id the web
nav lists without an ungated pane behind it. Its two limits are recorded at the
top of that file - it reasons about gate conditions by name rather than by
evaluating them, and it says nothing about what a pane draws.

Five sections are listed AND rendered. Each was measured pane by pane against the
deployed host; the per-section evidence is in `docs/audit/settings/`.

- Computer Use. On a non-Mac host the pane's own `platform !== 'darwin'` check
  drops the macOS permission block, leaving the Computer Use skill setup panel,
  which runs on `skills.discover` (real host data) and a working terminal. The
  nav's install badge is ungated with it, so the row and the pane agree.
- Mobile. `pairing-credentials.diff` wires mint and revoke for this pane, and its
  "To test" names Settings > Mobile.
- Browser. Its rows are settings-backed through `settings.update`, and
  `browser.screencast.v1` is advertised by `orca serve` on the host.
- Orca Account. The read half is real end to end: `orcaProfiles.authStatus` routes
  to the host and the pane draws the host's actual profile state, its status line
  and its capability rows. Sign-in still has to be started on the host console and
  the pane says so rather than blaming the host. Partial, not empty - and partial
  is not a reason to hide a capability behind a flag that means "desktop only".
- Plugins. `install`, `refresh` and `remove` are unrouted, and that is not what
  stops the pane: `PluginsSettingsSection` guards its list effect on
  `settings.pluginSystemEnabled`, and that key reaches no host, so the tile flips a
  browser-local boolean and the six methods `plugins-web-bridge.diff` already
  wired - list, consent, setEnabled, invokeCommand, readPanelEntry, panelAction -
  are never called at all. Routing that one key is its own change; the section has
  to be reachable for it to be worth making.

SSH Hosts stays out, and for a reason no pane inspection reaches. The SSH layer is
never installed under `orca serve`: `registerSshHandlers` and
`registerDaemonManagementHandlers` each have exactly one non-test caller, both in
`attach-main-window-services.ts`, and `orca serve` creates no BrowserWindow. So
`setSshTargetRegistryStore` never runs, `listTargets` answers `[]` and `connect`
throws `ssh_handlers_not_registered`. Listing the section would give a pane that
reports host state it never read - an empty-state card, an Import that says
"already in sync" without a round trip, a Remove that toasts success having done
nothing. That is worse than absent. What unhides it is installing that layer at
process level under serve, one hunk in `src/main/index.ts` beside the existing
`setPtyHostBindings` install; it is not settings work and it is not in this patch.

Mobile Emulator and macOS Permissions keep upstream's gate. The earlier
host-platform axis (`hostPlatform` from `status.get`) listed both to a browser
driving a Mac host, and neither pane was ever exercised over the wire. The axis
itself is real and wider than these two - `accounts` and all seven `integrations`
cards read the renderer's platform for a host property - but it is decided once,
in `shared/execution-host.ts`, not here.

One claim the v4.190.1 header made is withdrawn: `computer.capabilities` is called
by no renderer file, so listing Computer Use never surfaced its missing-package
message. And listing a section because its RPC family is registered is not
evidence the pane works - `ssh` has a registered family and an unusable pane.

Voice, Notifications and Advanced stay hidden for the reasons recorded at each
site: no `speech` namespace in the web preload, native toasts through a host port
that is inert with no renderer, and proxy keys the `SettingsUpdate` schema
rejects.

To test, open Settings in the tile: Computer Use, Mobile, Browser, Orca Account
and Plugins are listed and each opens onto a rendered pane; searching for "plugin"
or "orca account" finds them. SSH Hosts is not listed, and searching for "ssh"
finds nothing. On the desktop every section is listed exactly as before. Covered
by `settings-nav-web-parity.test.ts`, which pins the web and desktop nav both
ways, and `settings-nav-render-parity.test.ts`, which fails if the nav lists a
section `Settings.tsx` does not render ungated.
